text":"/d15"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"/d47","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":515.87044648,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":288.36008,"r":288.36008,"t":526.4607328000001},"charspan":[0,4],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/32","text":"/d47"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"/d47","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":516.547906,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":288.36008,"r":288.36008,"t":526.4607328000001},"charspan":[0,4],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/33","text":"/d47"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"/d47","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":515.8702204800001,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":330.47994,"r":330.47994,"t":526.4605068000001},"charspan":[0,4],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/34","text":"/d47"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"/d47","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":516.54768,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":330.47994,"r":330.47994,"t":526.4605068000001},"charspan":[0,4],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/35","text":"/d47"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"From Remark 6, we regard the cofibrations µ and t n as inclusions and consider that T n ( µ ) = n i =0 p -1 i ( A ) ⊆ Z n +1 h where p i is the i -th projection p i : Z n +1 h → Z h .","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":477.30361135,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":126.71981000000011,"r":485.45421249545706,"t":501.39178},"charspan":[0,183],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/36","text":"From Remark 6, we regard the cofibrations µ and t n as inclusions and consider that T n ( µ ) = n i =0 p -1 i ( A ) ⊆ Z n +1 h where p i is the i -th projection p i : Z n +1 h → Z h ."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"⋃ Assume we have the map Φ and the homotopy H : X × I → Z n +1 h between ∆ n +1 ◦ f ′ and t n ◦ Φ.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":447.90357134999994,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":126.71974000000017,"r":485.4092145175458,"t":489.8784456},"charspan":[0,98],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/37","text":"⋃ Assume we have the map Φ and the homotopy H : X × I → Z n +1 h between ∆ n +1 ◦ f ′ and t n ◦ Φ."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Consider the open neighborhood N of A in Z h which is deformable in Z h into A with some homotopy G rel A . For 0 ⩽ i ⩽ n , let h i = p i ◦ t n ◦ Φ and U i = h -1 ( N ).","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":424.14765609999995,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":126.71911000000011,"r":485.3110900000001,"t":446.80094599999995},"charspan":[0,169],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/38","text":"Consider the open neighborhood N of A in Z h which is deformable in Z h into A with some homotopy G rel A . For 0 ⩽ i ⩽ n , let h i = p i ◦ t n ◦ Φ and U i = h -1 ( N )."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":"L i ( u, t ) = { p i ( H ( u, 2 t )) if 0 ⩽ t ⩽ 1 2 G ( h i ( u ) , 2 t -1) if 1 2 ⩽ t ⩽ 1 .","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":363.30351135,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":213.11914000000002,"r":397.67957128000006,"t":393.3990856},"charspan":[0,92],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/39","text":""},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"i As ( t n ◦ Φ)( X ) ⊆ T n ( µ ) = ⋃ n i =0 p -1 i ( A ), we have X = ⋃ n i =0 U i . Now we can define homotopies L i : U i × I → Z h by:","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":392.5605782399999,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":126.7190500000001,"r":457.2565454000001,"t":429.29906889999995},"charspan":[0,137],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/40","text":"i As ( t n ◦ Φ)( X ) ⊆ T n ( µ ) = ⋃ n i =0 p -1 i ( A ), we have X = ⋃ n i =0 U i . Now we can define homotopies L i : U i × I → Z h by:"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Each L i is well defined because p i ( H ( u, 1)) = p i (( t n ◦ Φ)( u )) = h i ( u ) = G ( h i ( u ) , 0). We have L i ( u, 0) = f ′ ( u ) and L i ( u, 1) ∈ A , so L i is a homotopy between f ′ | U i and µ ◦ l i where l i = L i (-, 1).","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":321.9077201,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":126.7197799999999,"r":485.40909329365223,"t":356.44110600000005},"charspan":[0,236],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/41","text":"Each L i is well defined because p i ( H ( u, 1)) = p i (( t n ◦ Φ)( u )) = h i ( u ) = G ( h i ( u ) , 0). We have L i ( u, 0) = f ′ ( u ) and L i ( u, 1) ∈ A , so L i is a homotopy between f ′ | U i and µ ◦ l i where l i = L i (-, 1)."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Conversely assume we have an open cover ( U i ) 0 ⩽ i ⩽ n of X and homotopies K i : U i × I → Z h with K i ( u, 0) = f ′ ( u ) and K i ( u, 1) ∈ A . Choose a closed subset F i ⊂ U i (possibly the empty set) in each U i . If X is normal, we have two more open covers ( V i ) 0 ⩽ i ⩽ n and ( W i ) 0 ⩽ i ⩽ n of X such that","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":267.5437353499999,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":126.71919999999994,"r":485.3826648170921,"t":314.072144},"charspan":[0,320],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/42","text":"Conversely assume we have an open cover ( U i ) 0 ⩽ i ⩽ n of X and homotopies K i : U i × I → Z h with K i ( u, 0) = f ′ ( u ) and K i ( u, 1) ∈ A . Choose a closed subset F i ⊂ U i (possibly the empty set) in each U i . If X is normal, we have two more open covers ( V i ) 0 ⩽ i ⩽ n and ( W i ) 0 ⩽ i ⩽ n of X such that"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":"F i ⊂ W i ⊂ W i ⊂ V i ⊂ V i ⊂ U i .","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":248.9477561,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":239.63926999999995,"r":372.35961128,"t":259.60131},"charspan":[0,35],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/43","text":""},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Moreover, as W i and ∁ V i = X \\ V i are disjoint closed subspaces of the normal space X , we have an Urysohn map ϕ i : X → I with ϕ i ( W i ) = 1 and ϕ i ( ∁ V i ) = 0.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":218.34755610000002,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":126.71996000000001,"r":485.38239286340576,"t":242.07835608000005},"charspan":[0,169],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/44","text":"Moreover, as W i and ∁ V i = X \\ V i are disjoint closed subspaces of the normal space X , we have an Urysohn map ϕ i : X → I with ϕ i ( W i ) = 1 and ϕ i ( ∁ V i ) = 0."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Now we define H i : X × I → Z h by:","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":206.4676561,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":138.60026,"r":294.0000307136272,"t":217.120946},"charspan":[0,35],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/45","text":"Now we define H i : X × I → Z h by:"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":"H i ( x, t ) = { f ′ ( x ) if x ∈ ∁ V i K i ( x, tϕ i ( x )) if x ∈ U i .","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":170.3204482399999,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":224.40021000000002,"r":385.85698540000004,"t":200.1984056},"charspan":[0,73],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/46","text":""},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Each H i is well defined because if x ∈ ∁ V i ∩ U i , then K i ( x, tϕ i ( x )) = K i ( x, 0) = f ′ ( x ).","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":139.70934,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":126.71929000000003,"r":485.3411029460759,"t":162.63831607999998},"charspan":[0,106],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/47","text":"Each H i is well defined because if x ∈ ∁ V i ∩ U i , then K i ( x, tϕ i ( x )) = K i ( x, 0) = f ′ ( x )."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Let H = ( H 0 , . . . , H n ): X × I → Z n +1 h and Φ = H (-, 1). We have H (-, 0) = ∆ n +1 ◦ f ′ . On the other hand, for any x ∈ X , there is (at least) one i such that x ∈ W i , so ϕ i ( x ) = 1 and H i ( x, 1) = K i ( x, 1) ∈ A ; this means that Φ( X ) ⊆ T n ( µ ). Thus H is a homotopy between ∆ n +1 ◦ f ′ and t n ◦ Φ. □","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":90.90377134999994,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":126.72052999999988,"r":485.34138127999984,"t":139.51050750000002},"charspan":[0,326],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/48","text":"Let H = ( H 0 , . . . , H n ): X × I → Z n +1 h and Φ = H (-, 1). We have H (-, 0) = ∆ n +1 ◦ f ′ . On the other hand, for any x ∈ X , there is (at least) one i such that x ∈ W i , so ϕ i ( x ) = 1 and H i ( x, 1) = K i ( x, 1) ∈ A ; this means that Φ( X ) ⊆ T n ( µ ). Thus H is a homotopy between ∆ n +1 ◦ f ′ and t n ◦ Φ. □"}],"version":"1.10.0"}